Committed as 38b398d1.

## What happened

Reproduced first, fixed second. Before the change, cargo test --bin yoyo apply_effort_hint failed 6 of 12 parallel runs and 0 of 6 single-threaded. After: 12/12 and 6/6 green. Both numbers are in the commit message, because one green run proves nothing against a 6-in-12 bug.

The mechanism was exactly as the task described: three tests each writing EFFORT_LEVEL, a process-global AtomicU8, on libtest's parallel threads. _medium_noop's store lands between _low_prepends's store and its read, the low test sees Medium's empty hint, contains("concise") is false.

The fix is injection, not scheduling. `apply_effort_hint_with(level, input) holds the whole old body; apply_effort_hint(input)` is a one-line wrapper reading the global. Production is byte-identical. All three test names and every assertion survived; they now pass an explicit EffortLevel and touch no global. No `#[serial]` — it only serialises against other #[serial] tests, so it would leave the class alive for the next test and leave a false record of which tests are dangerous.

## Two judgment calls worth flagging

I ran the positive control. My own archive says a discriminator tested only on the side that passes is vacuous green — so I hardcoded the wrapper to Medium and confirmed the new source-level guard actually fails with the right message, then restored. It's a weak check and the doc comment says so outright: it proves the call is present, not that its result is used. The pure half carries the real assertions.

I declined the optional tail, on evidence. run_prompt_with_content_and_changes builds [Effort: {hint}] as a separate Content::Text block — not the same string as the wrapper's [Effort: {hint}]\n\n{input}. Unifying them would change what reaches the model, so the duplication stands and CLAUDE.md now records that the second path is untested rather than quietly leaving it looking handled.

## What I won't claim

This proves the class is gone at this site. The three mutation readings in CLAUDE.md are caveated on "the flaky class is not proven gone repo-wide" — and they still should be. One cured site is not a repo-wide proof, and the temporal link between the 16:06 red and that session's revert remains unverified.
